Durarara!! AU
Chapter 19 – Part 2
Curtis did not look back.
He only nodded once, almost imperceptibly, and kept walking. Long black braids shifted against the black compression shirt as his figure moved into the ordinary flow of the sidewalk, step after steady step, until the crowd and the distance began to take him in pieces — shoulder, braid, dark fabric, then less, then nothing she could honestly still call within reach.
Anri remained where she had stopped.
For a moment the correct shape of the exchange still held: he had asked to be alone, she had agreed, she had not chased. Responsible. Respectful. Mature. Then the quieter consequence arrived underneath all that and twisted hard in her chest.
I messed up.
The thought was immediate and total. If she had managed her face better after the fight — if she had spoken sooner, more cleanly, without letting fear sit in her eyes long enough for him to read it as disgust — he might not have vanished into the crowd in the first place. He might not have climbed whatever height he had needed to climb to get away from the stare. He might not be walking away from her now with that defeated look still clinging to the line of his shoulders.
Around her, Ikebukuro continued like the afternoon had only ever been errands and canceled classes. Anri stood inside it feeling the opposite of continuous. She had told him he was not a freak. She had told him the look was her failure. She had promised she would still be there when he could stand company again.
None of that stopped the hard, private certainty settling under her ribs:
She had been given one fragile chance to correct the damage, and the correction had still ended with his back turned and the distance returning, quiet and self-inflicted and entirely possible to blame on the way she had first stared at the boy who protected her.
A few blocks farther on, when Anri’s voice had fully left the air behind him, Curtis finally let his shoulders drop. Long black braids shifted against the black compression shirt as he tipped his head back toward the washed-out afternoon sky and exhaled through his teeth.
“…So stupid.” The words were quiet, meant for no one. “Why do I let myself get attached.”
Rio.
Anri.
Celty.
Every time he allowed a soft point of contact, the city turned it into consequence. Stares. Expectations. Fear. Doors that opened whether he was ready for the rooms behind them or not. He should have stayed a list and a ghost. Instead he kept answering people like the answering might not eventually cost blood, distance, or the look in someone’s eyes when the whole of him showed through.
His phone rang.
Curtis closed his eyes once, then brought it up without checking the name.
“…Hello?”
“Curtis.”
Celty’s real voice came through the line as clearly as if she were standing in the street beside him — not speaker-thin, not filtered into ordinary phone sound, but the private channel’s impossible clarity riding the call anyway. Even over the phone, he could hear her. The same voice from the roof. The same voice from the morning flat. The same voice that had asked whether she could stay by him and then followed him into the consequences of being heard.
For a second he only stood there with the sky above him and that voice in his ear, the frustration in his chest colliding with the one attachment he had not managed to outwalk yet.
Curtis kept his eyes on the sidewalk ahead, long black braids shifting against the black compression shirt as he forced his voice flatter than he felt.
“Yup. Speaking.”
There was a short pause on the line, the kind that meant Celty was reading his tone as carefully as the words.
Then her real voice came through again, clear and close despite the distance.
“You didn’t answer my texts.” Not an accusation yet — a placement. “I saw the fight video. The black shirt. The braids. The way that man hit you and nothing happened. Then the ending.” A thinner beat. “I’m not calling to ask for a public explanation. I’m calling because you look like someone who walked away from more than a street fight.”
Another pause, lower.
“Where are you right now. And don’t say ‘nowhere’ or ‘it’s fine.’ You’ve used those already today in ways I don’t trust.” The private clarity of her voice hardened just slightly. “If you’re about to climb another roof and turn the whole afternoon into smoke and self-erasure, I want to know before you finish doing it.”
In the background of the call, somewhere on her end, the city moved around the Black Rider on a break she was clearly no longer treating as rest.
Curtis kept walking as he listened, long black braids shifting against the black compression shirt, the calm mask doing only partial work against the residual defeat in his posture.
“…I’m on the street. Heading home.” A short pause. “Already did the roof version earlier. You missed that show.”
The attempt at dryness landed thin. He exhaled through his nose and continued before she could pin only that piece of it.
“There was a public fight. Some imported tough guy putting locals on the pavement and running his mouth. He went at Anri-chan with an ugly name. I stepped in. Gave him a chance to apologize. He didn’t take it.” Curtis’s jaw tightened around the memory of the crack. “Afterward Anri looked at me like she’d just watched something she couldn’t put back in a safe category. I left. She found me again. I told her I needed to be alone. That’s where you called into.”
Another quieter beat, more honest than he preferred.
“I’m not climbing anything right now. I’m just… done being stared at for one afternoon. Done being useful, frightening, and then watched like the useful part doesn’t redeem the frightening part.” His mismatched eyes stayed on the sidewalk. “You don’t have to come check on me. I’m going home. Door, lock, silence. That’s the whole plan.”
He almost left it there, then added, lower:
“…And yes. It was me in the video.”
Celty was quiet for a second after he finished, the private clarity of the line carrying only the faint undercurrent of city noise on her end.
Then her real voice returned, measured and firm.
“Of course it was you in the video. That part was never the question.” A short pause. “The question was whether the person who walked out of that circle still had somewhere to put the aftermath besides a locked room and a dead phone.”
Another beat, lower.
“I understand why Anri’s look cut you. People stare at me the same way when the helmet comes off or the shadow shows too much. Like the useful version was tolerable, but the full version needs quarantine.” The line held steady. “That doesn’t make the stare righteous. It also doesn’t mean disappearing is the only correct response to it.”
Her voice softened only slightly — not enough to become gentle theater, just enough to leave the door unbarred.
“Go home if you need the walls. Lock the door if you need the lock. I’m not going to argue you into company you already refused.” A thinner pause. “But don’t turn the phone face-down on the whole city like every person in it has already failed the same test. I called because I saw the fight and the exit after it. That is not the same thing as staring at you like a freak.”
In the background, Shooter’s idle engine murmured under her words.
“If the silence starts turning toxic instead of useful… use the door you opened this morning. I meant what I said about coming back. That offer didn’t expire because the afternoon got ugly.”
Curtis stopped at the edge of a low embankment path where the water ran dull and restless under the afternoon light. Long black braids shifted against the black compression shirt as he closed his eyes once and let the last useful piece of the conversation land.
“…Thanks, Celty.” His voice came out quieter, stripped of the earlier edge. “I really mean it.”
He ended the call before the sincerity could become another attachment point he had to manage.
For a second he only stood there with the dead line in his hand, the city continuing around a man who had already asked too many people for understanding in one day and then flinched when the understanding arrived complicated. Anri’s corrected fear. Celty’s refusal to treat silence as a complete solution. The residual public noise of a fight video already traveling farther than his patience could tolerate.
Curtis looked at the phone.
Then he threw it into the water.
The splash was small. Final. The current took the dark shape under and erased the rest in a few indifferent seconds. No more calls. No more texts. No more soft voices arriving in real time to test whether he could stand being reached.
He turned away from the embankment and walked home with his hands in his pockets, mismatched eyes lowered, the route ordinary and the decision under it anything but. By the time the flat door closed behind him, the Chief of the North, the café listener, the rooftop confessor, and the boy who kept answering women when they needed someone had all been reduced to one practical fact:
He was alone on purpose now.
And the city would have to find another way in.
Evening settled over the flat in slow, ordinary layers.
Curtis sat on the couch with a warm mug of coffee between his palms, long black braids resting against the black compression shirt, the city reduced to a muted presence beyond the windows. Beside him, a guitar leaned against the cushion within easy reach — not as performance, not as public confession, just an object from the quieter version of his hands. He had not turned on the television. He had not checked a screen. The locked door and the cooling light were doing enough work for now.
He drank.
He breathed.
He let the afternoon’s residual violence and unfinished conversations thin out in the stillness.
What he did not realize — could not realize, with the phone already claimed by the current — was how busy the silence was on the other side of it.
Anri called first, then again, then stopped calling long enough to send another string of careful messages that would never mark as delivered on any device he still owned. Rio tried after that, lighter at first, then more uncertain when the ring never connected. Celty, between courier routes, called once with the same private clarity as before and got only the void where a line should have opened. She tried again. The result did not change.
No ring reached the flat.
No vibration crossed the couch.
No notification interrupted the steam rising from his coffee.
To Curtis, the evening was finally still.
To the three women trying to reach him, it was an unanswered door with no way to tell whether he was only quiet… or already gone farther than a single afternoon’s request to be alone.
He set the mug down, rested a hand on the guitar’s neck without playing yet, and stared at the dimming room as if solitude were a structure that could hold.
Outside, their calls kept failing into the empty space where his phone no longer existed.
Anri
The call failed again, and Anri felt the failure land lower than panic — somewhere closer to guilt with nowhere to put itself. She stood under a station light with the phone hard in both hands, replaying the street, the crack, the defeated look on Curtis’s face when he asked to be alone. She had tried to correct the stare. She had tried to tell him he was not a freak. And still the line would not open. In her chest the same thought kept returning with worse clarity each time: he asked for space, and I am already turning that space into proof that I damaged something I cannot reach back into. The unanswered calls did not feel neutral. They felt like a door she had helped close by looking at him wrong when it mattered.

Rio
Rio stared at the silent screen with a different kind of unease — lighter on the surface, more restless underneath. Curtis not answering was not automatically catastrophe in her experience; he wandered, he went quiet, he lived by his own weather. But no ring at all made the afternoon feel strangely hollow. She had only wanted to know if he was free, if the canceled day might still include him, if the boy who had caught her and later given her his number was somewhere ordinary and reachable. The longer the failure held, the more her thoughts drifted toward the worst soft possibilities: he was upset, he was hurt, he had decided people were too much again. Don’t disappear just because the city got heavy, she thought, almost irritated with how quickly fondness turned into worry. You said I could text when things got hard. What am I supposed to do when you’re the one who vanishes?

Celty
Celty’s reaction was colder and more precise, which did not make it smaller. On a narrow service road between deliveries, yellow helmet angled down toward the PDA, she listened to the call fail and felt the morning’s open door slam into an unexpected wall. He had thanked her. He had said he meant it. Then the line went dead in a way that no longer felt like fatigue alone. Curtis asking to be alone she could understand. Curtis becoming completely unreachable after a public explosion of violence and several emotional collisions in one day sat differently under the helmet. Black smoke pressed uselessly against the seal as the private thought arrived hard and unwelcome: If the phone is not even ringing, then either he is already past the point of wanting contact… or he has removed the means of contact entirely. Neither option comforted her. Both made the invitation from that morning feel suddenly less like a standing door and more like a promise that could be drowned under the same impulse that sent him up ledges and out of crowds when the staring started.
